# kex: run-beam
# A `serving` process's slot, called through a `Server<T>` value whose
# owning module `T` is never `using`-imported by the caller — reached only
# through full qualification (`Catalog.Shelf.Stock`) or, as here, a record
# field typed `Server<Catalog.Shelf.Stock>`.
#
# `checkCall`'s candidate filter exempts a make-block method from needing an
# explicit import when "a value whose TYPE names that module" already
# supplies the qualification (`CollisionWeb.Server.build(...)` needs no
# `using CollisionWeb` for the same reason). That exemption reads the
# receiver's OWN type name to find its module — correct for an ordinary,
# unwrapped receiver, but `Process.spawn`'s result is `Server<T>`, not `T`:
# the receiver's name is the bare word "Server", which has no module prefix
# at all, so the exemption always missed it and fell through to requiring
# `T`'s module be imported after all — even though the call already spells
# it out in full. With no candidate left, the slot call fell all the way
# through to the runtime's generic method dispatcher, which has no case for
# a `Server` handle: `kex -C` reports nothing wrong, and the first real call
# dies with `Undefined method: filed for Server`. Fixed by unwrapping
# `Server<T>` to `T` before reading the module prefix, same as an ordinary
# receiver already gets.
#
# BEAM-only: the interpreter dispatches every call dynamically and never
# consulted this candidate list in the first place.
module Catalog.Shelf do
  record Stock do
    count : Integer = 0
  end

  serving Stock do
    slot filed() -> Reply<Integer> do
      new.count = @count + 1
      return { new, reply: new.count }
    end
  end
end

record Handle do
  process : Server<Catalog.Shelf.Stock>
end

main do
  let handle = Handle { process: Process.spawn(Catalog.Shelf.Stock {}) }
  IO.printLine("${handle.process.filed().try}")
  IO.printLine("${handle.process.filed().try}")
end
